FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_P12
VAR_INPUT
    THICKNESS_FAULT : BOOL;
    THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID : BOOL;
    CUTTING_RUN : BOOL;
    FaultTimestamp : DINT;
    CycleTime : TIME;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    THICKNESS_ALARM : BOOL;
    Slab_Fault_Counter : UINT;
    Slab_Runtime_Accumulator : LTIME;
    Slab_Fault_History : ARRAY[0..4] OF DINT;
END_VAR
VAR
    StartupScans : USINT := 0;
    PreviousValidFault : BOOL := FALSE;
    FaultRising : BOOL := FALSE;
    HoldActive : BOOL := FALSE;
    HoldElapsed : TIME := T#0ms;

    InternalCounter : UINT := 0;
    InternalRuntime : LTIME := LTIME#0ns;
    InternalHistory : ARRAY[0..4] OF DINT := [0, 0, 0, 0, 0];

    RuntimeIncrement : LTIME := LTIME#0ns;
    MaximumRuntime : LTIME := LTIME#9223372036854775807ns;
    Index : INT;
END_VAR

(*
Requirement text:
Monitor slab-thickness faults on a stone-cutting line, including minimum
alarm holding, fault counting, normal-cutting runtime accumulation and
a history of the five most recent faults.

1. When THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID is TRUE, THICKNESS_FAULT indicates that
thickness deviation exceeds tolerance. Activate THICKNESS_ALARM immediately
when the fault becomes TRUE, and hold each triggered alarm for at least
500 ms. Keep the alarm active while the fault persists.
2. On each FALSE-to-TRUE transition of a valid fault signal, increment
Slab_Fault_Counter and store the current FaultTimestamp in element 0 of
Slab_Fault_History. Shift older entries toward higher indices, retaining
only the five most recent entries.
3. Accumulate Slab_Runtime_Accumulator using CycleTime only while CUTTING_RUN
is TRUE, the thickness signal is valid and no thickness fault is present.
Otherwise preserve the accumulated value.
4. At power-up or whenever THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID is FALSE, switch off
the alarm and clear its holding state. Do not add fault records or
accumulate normal runtime while the signal is invalid.
5. At power-up, initialize the fault count, accumulated runtime and all
five history entries to zero. Statistics and history are for observation
and maintenance only; they must not alter the cutting equipment's
operating permissive, and external callers must not modify history entries.

Formal specification retained:
C2: G(THICKNESS_ALARM -> THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID)
C5: G(THICKNESS_FAULT && THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID ->
      F[0,25](THICKNESS_ALARM))
C6: G(THICKNESS_ALARM -> (THICKNESS_ALARM W !THICKNESS_FAULT))
C8: G(!THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID -> X(!THICKNESS_ALARM))
C10: G[0,1](!THICKNESS_ALARM)

PENDING SEMANTIC REVIEW:
- C10 literally requires alarm FALSE at scan indices 0 and 1. This conflicts
  with immediate activation if a valid fault is present at startup.
  This implementation gives the explicit startup interval priority.
- C6 conflicts with C2 when validity is lost while the raw fault remains
  TRUE. Invalid-signal clearing takes priority here.
- C8 literally also requires the next scan to remain alarm-free following
  an invalid scan, even if validity returns with a fault. This conflicts
  with immediate activation. This implementation clears on the invalid
  scan and handles a restored valid fault immediately.
- C6 does not itself imply a 500 ms hold after a short fault.
  The separate holding state below implements that textual requirement.
- UINT cannot represent unlimited occurrences. At 65535 the counter
  saturates; history continues recording. Exact unbounded counting is
  impossible with the specified interface.
- Runtime saturates at the supported signed 64-bit nanosecond LTIME limit.
  Nonpositive CycleTime is rejected without altering accumulated runtime.
- VAR_OUTPUT does not enforce caller access permissions in portable ST.
  Private history is authoritative and outputs are republished each scan.
  Strict external write prohibition requires project access configuration.

Compilation profile must support LTIME, TIME_TO_LTIME, the LTIME literals
used here, and standard array initialization. No compilation or functional
verification is claimed. Internal state must be non-retentive at power-up.
Each invocation represents 20 ms; CycleTime is used for runtime only.
*)

FaultRising := FALSE;

IF StartupScans < 2 THEN
    StartupScans := StartupScans + 1;

    THICKNESS_ALARM := FALSE;
    PreviousValidFault := FALSE;
    HoldActive := FALSE;
    HoldElapsed := T#0ms;

    InternalCounter := 0;
    InternalRuntime := LTIME#0ns;
    FOR Index := 0 TO 4 DO
        InternalHistory[Index] := 0;
    END_FOR;

ELSIF NOT THICKNESS_SIGNAL_VALID THEN
    THICKNESS_ALARM := FALSE;
    PreviousValidFault := FALSE;
    HoldActive := FALSE;
    HoldElapsed := T#0ms;

ELSE
    FaultRising := THICKNESS_FAULT AND NOT PreviousValidFault;

    IF FaultRising THEN
        IF InternalCounter < UINT#65535 THEN
            InternalCounter := InternalCounter + UINT#1;
        END_IF;

        FOR Index := 4 TO 1 BY -1 DO
            InternalHistory[Index] := InternalHistory[Index - 1];
        END_FOR;
        InternalHistory[0] := FaultTimestamp;

        HoldActive := TRUE;
        HoldElapsed := T#0ms;

    ELSIF HoldActive THEN
        IF HoldElapsed < T#500ms THEN
            HoldElapsed := HoldElapsed + T#20ms;
        END_IF;

        IF HoldElapsed >= T#500ms THEN
            HoldElapsed := T#500ms;
            HoldActive := FALSE;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;

    THICKNESS_ALARM := THICKNESS_FAULT OR HoldActive;
    PreviousValidFault := THICKNESS_FAULT;

    IF CUTTING_RUN AND NOT THICKNESS_FAULT AND (CycleTime > T#0ms) THEN
        RuntimeIncrement := TIME_TO_LTIME(CycleTime);

        IF RuntimeIncrement > LTIME#0ns THEN
            IF RuntimeIncrement <= (MaximumRuntime - InternalRuntime) THEN
                InternalRuntime := InternalRuntime + RuntimeIncrement;
            ELSE
                InternalRuntime := MaximumRuntime;
            END_IF;
        END_IF;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

Slab_Fault_Counter := InternalCounter;
Slab_Runtime_Accumulator := InternalRuntime;
FOR Index := 0 TO 4 DO
    Slab_Fault_History[Index] := InternalHistory[Index];
END_FOR;

END_FUNCTION_BLOCK
